{"version": 2, "width": 110, "height": 24, "timestamp": 1794535326, "title": "dev/url-shortener-architecture \u00b7 not-ambitious run 1", "env": {"TERM": "xterm-256color"}, "theme": {"fg": "#d8dee9", "bg": "#1e1f1c", "palette": "#272822:#f92672:#a6e22e:#f4bf75:#66d9ef:#ae81ff:#a1efe4:#f8f8f2:#75715e:#f92672:#a6e22e:#f4bf75:#66d9ef:#ae81ff:#a1efe4:#f9f8f5"}}
[0.0, "o", "\u001b[?25l"]
[0.0, "o", "\r\n\u001b[1m\u001b[97m❯ Design the architecture for a URL shortener that handles 10k requests a second, as a Markdown file\u001b[0m\r\n"]
[40.707, "o", "\r\n\u001b[32m⏺\u001b[0m \u001b[1mWrote 1 file\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[90m  ⎿  Write(<workspace>/url-shortener-architecture.md)\u001b[0m\r\n"]
[45.837, "o", "\r\n\u001b[97m⏺ I wrote the design to `<workspace>/url-shortener-architecture.md`. I haven't load-tested it or run any of\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  it, so the sizing numbers are estimates.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  \u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  The main decisions in it:\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  \u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  - **Load assumption:** about 100:1 reads to writes, so roughly 9,900 redirects/s and 100 creates/s. At that\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  scale the design is mostly about keeping reads off the database.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  - **Read path:** CDN, then an in-process cache, then Redis, then the database. This should serve over 95% of\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  redirects from cache, with negative caching to absorb scans for codes that don't exist.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  - **Redirect type:** it defaults to 302 with a short `max-age`, so clicks still get counted and links can\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  still be edited or disabled. 301 is an opt-in.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  - **Short codes:** each Create instance gets a block of IDs, scrambles them with a reversible keyed\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  function, and encodes them as 7-character base62. That avoids collisions and per-write coordination, and the\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  codes aren't guessable. Custom aliases use a conditional insert.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  - **Storage:** a partitioned key-value store such as DynamoDB, Cassandra or Scylla, since every lookup is by\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  code. Sharded Postgres or MySQL is listed as an alternative.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  - **Analytics:** click events go asynchronously through Kafka into ClickHouse, so the redirect never waits\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  on them.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  - **Services:** redirect and create run as separate stateless services, so each scales and fails\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  independently.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  - **Sizing:** the plan covers about 3× headroom (30k req/s), with instance counts per component.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  \u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  The file also covers capacity estimates, the API, failure handling, abuse prevention, monitoring, a test\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  plan and future extensions.\u001b[0m\r\n"]
[50.401, "o", "\r\n\u001b[1m\u001b[97m❯ Push it further.\u001b[0m\r\n"]
[70.518, "o", "\r\n\u001b[97m⏺ I'll extend the document with a second part that goes deeper on the parts the first pass skimmed: failure\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  modes, consistency edge cases, the code-generation internals, hot keys, multi-region, schemas and rollout.\u001b[0m\r\n"]
[136.643, "o", "\r\n\u001b[32m⏺\u001b[0m \u001b[1mEdited 1 file\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[90m  ⎿  Edit(<workspace>/url-shortener-architecture.md)\u001b[0m\r\n"]
[143.949, "o", "\r\n\u001b[97m⏺ I added a Part II (sections 17–31) to `<workspace>/url-shortener-architecture.md`. It covers the areas the\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  first version skimmed. Nothing has been load-tested; the throughput and latency numbers are estimates.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  \u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  **What's new**\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  - **Latency budget and handler logic (§17–18):** a per-hop budget for the 50 ms p99 target, and pseudocode\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  for the redirect handler with request coalescing, jittered TTLs and non-blocking analytics.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  - **Code generation (§19):**\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m    - A 40-bit, 20+20 Feistel network maps counter values onto 7-character codes. That gives about 1.1T codes,\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  so the worst case of ~15B links uses roughly 1.4% of the space.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m    - ID block leasing keeps working for about 50 minutes if the allocator goes down.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m    - Aliases are kept structurally distinct from generated codes, so they can never collide.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  - **Key rotation:** changing the Feistel key would remap future counters onto codes already issued, causing\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  collisions. The doc says never to rotate it, and keeps a conditional insert on every create as a backstop.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  - **Consistency edge cases (§20):**\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m    - A create-then-click race, where a stale negative-cache entry would 404 a brand-new alias, is handled by\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  write-through plus negative-cache deletion.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m    - Takedown propagates through every cache layer, with a worst case under 60 seconds.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  - **Hot keys and stampedes (§21):** five layers of defence for a viral link, plus a plan for a full Redis\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  loss.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  - **Multi-region (§22):** disjoint ID ranges per region, a single alias authority, and per-region capacity\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  sized so losing one of three regions needs no scale-up.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  - **Schemas (§23):** concrete Cassandra, Postgres and ClickHouse definitions.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  - **Failure and security analysis (§24–25):**\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m    - A failure-mode table covers detection, impact, automatic response and recovery.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m    - The 99.99% target works out to a 4.3 minute monthly error budget.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m    - A threat model covers abuse, SSRF and enumeration.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  - **Operations and decisions (§26–31):**\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m    - Burn-rate alerts and synthetic probes, a headroom table with the main cost drivers, and runbooks,\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  deploys and resharding.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m    - Alternatives that were rejected, and a four-phase rollout.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m    - Seven open questions to settle before building.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  \u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  The doc says plainly that Postgres, Redis and a CDN are enough at 10k req/s. The partitioned store and\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  multi-region work are later phases, added only when a measured need appears.\u001b[0m\r\n"]
